{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatioExtensivity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatioExtensivity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic"],"declarations":28,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.pairwise_additive_cannot_reach_alpha","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.pairwise_additive_cannot_reach_alpha","kind":"theorem","line":737,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L737-L768","doc":"**No additive pairwise model on any topology reaches the alpha binding.**\n\n`sPN` is fixed by `2H`; connectivity of the calibrating nuclei bounds `sPP` and\n`sNN`; the edge counts are capped by what the `4He` nucleon multiset permits\n(one pp, four pn, one nn). The conclusion is strict, so no boundary case\nescapes.\n\nHypotheses are stated as bounds rather than equations precisely so that the\ntheorem covers *every* graph at once rather than one enumeration.\n\nNote `0 ≤ sPP` and `0 ≤ sNN` are **not** needed and are deliberately absent:\nthe compiler flagged them unused, and dropping them strengthens the result to\ncover negative bond values too. Physically the bond values are positive, so the\nextra generality is free rather than useful — but an unused hypothesis is worth\nremoving, not silencing. This is the second time in this file the linter has\nfound a hypothesis the proof does not use. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chain_additive_cannot_match_alpha","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chain_additive_cannot_match_alpha","kind":"theorem","line":673,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L673-L685","doc":"**Parameter-free refutation.** If a pairwise-additive chain model matches the\nAME bindings of `2H`, `3H` and `3He`, its `4He` binding is forced to\n`13.975275` MeV and therefore cannot be the AME value `28.295661`.\n\nNo per-bond value appears in the conclusion: the hypotheses pin three numbers and\n`alpha_relation` determines the fourth, whatever `sPP, sPN, sNN` are. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.alpha_relation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.alpha_relation","kind":"theorem","line":663,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L663-L671","doc":"The bond-multiset identity, as a relation among bindings. Holds for **every**\nchoice of per-bond values — they cancel. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chainBinding","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chainBinding","kind":"def","line":658,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L658-L661","doc":"Chain binding as an additive functional on the bond multiset, parametrised\nby `p = Z-1` and `n = N-1` so no `ℕ` subtraction appears. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.bounded_not_eventually_above_line","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.bounded_not_eventually_above_line","kind":"theorem","line":551,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L551-L568","doc":"The `g ≤ 1` half of the ASYMPTOTIC exclusion, proved: a bounded channel\ncannot dominate a positive line, because a line is unbounded.\n\nStated finitely — given any `c > 0` there is a bond count at which the line has\novertaken the channel — so no filters are needed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chain_ge_geom_iterated","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chain_ge_geom_iterated","kind":"theorem","line":537,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L537-L549","doc":"The iteration the §6 docstring asserted without proving: for `g ≥ 0` and\n`f ∈ [0,1]`, `m` steps multiply the channel by at least `g ^ m`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chainB_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chainB_step","kind":"theorem","line":518,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L518-L535","doc":"One step, stated natively for `chainB` so the iteration below needs no\n`simpa` normalisation (which is what made the first attempt miss an off-by-one\nin the `g` exponent: `chainB`'s is `b+1`, not `b`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chain_never_exactly_linear","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chain_never_exactly_linear","kind":"theorem","line":470,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L470-L516","doc":"**The exclusion, proved.** The channel is never *exactly* linear in the bond\ncount, for any `S, g > 0` and any `f < 1`. Uses only `b = 1, 2, 3`.\n\nNote `0 < f` is **not** needed and is deliberately absent: the compiler flagged\nit unused, and dropping it strengthens the statement to cover `f ≤ 0` as well.\nThe physical `f` is a bond factor in `(0,1)`, so the extra generality is free\nrather than useful — but an unused hypothesis is worth removing, not\nsilencing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chainB","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chainB","kind":"def","line":467,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L467-L468","doc":"The channel, indexed by bond count `b` (so `A = b + 1` nucleons). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.logRatio_independent_of_growth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.logRatio_independent_of_growth","kind":"theorem","line":398,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L398-L406","doc":"**The mechanism.** The log-ratio is independent of the free-trace growth\n`g`: it cancels between numerator and denominator. The difference channel of §6\ncannot cancel it, which is exactly why that one is never extensive and this one\nalways is. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.logRatio_linear_in_bonds","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.logRatio_linear_in_bonds","kind":"theorem","line":386,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L386-L396","doc":"The log-ratio of free to bonded trace is **exactly linear in the bond\ncount**, with slope `-log f`. This is the derivation behind the corpus's\n\"the extensive invariant is the log-ratio\". ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chain_step_ge_growth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chain_step_ge_growth","kind":"theorem","line":322,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L322-L340","doc":"For `g > 1` and `f ∈ [0,1)`, each step multiplies the channel by at least\n`g`. Iterating, `B n ≥ B 0 * g ^ n` — at least geometric growth, which a linear\nfunction cannot match. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chain_bounded_of_growth_le_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.chain_bounded_of_growth_le_one","kind":"theorem","line":311,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L311-L320","doc":"For `g ≤ 1` and `f ∈ [0,1]`, the chain channel is bounded by `S`. Bounded\nmeans not linear, so this branch cannot be extensive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.sum_ge_card_mul_last","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.sum_ge_card_mul_last","kind":"theorem","line":250,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L250-L261","doc":"For an antitone marginal sequence, the running average dominates the last\nmarginal. So an average that has fallen by `x %` sits on a marginal that has\nfallen by *at least* `x %` — the average is a lagging indicator, never a\nleading one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.card_large_marginals_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.card_large_marginals_le","kind":"theorem","line":231,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L231-L242","doc":"The usable form: at most `M / ε` of the first `n` marginals reach `ε`.\n\nThis is the bound that actually excludes a nonzero limit — if `m(A) → c > 0`\nthen *every* sufficiently large `A` has `m(A) ≥ c/2`, i.e. unboundedly many,\ncontradicting the fixed ceiling `M / (c/2)`. Unlike the multiplicative form\nabove it genuinely needs `0 < ε`, which is why both are stated. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.card_large_marginals_mul_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.card_large_marginals_mul_le","kind":"theorem","line":200,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L200-L229","doc":"If the marginals are nonnegative and every partial sum is at most `M`, then\nfor any `ε > 0` the number of marginals reaching `ε` is bounded: their count\ntimes `ε` is at most `M`.\n\nConsequence for the binding: a **bounded** total (which is what \"the binding is\nintensive\" says) permits only boundedly many marginals above any threshold, so\n`m(A) → c > 0` is impossible. Both chains' marginals therefore go to zero, and\nthe measured question is only *how fast*. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.one_sub_sum_le_prod_one_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.one_sub_sum_le_prod_one_sub","kind":"theorem","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L172-L192","doc":"`1 - ∑_{i<n} a i ≤ ∏_{i<n} (1 - a i)` for `a i ∈ [0,1]`.\n\nRead with `a i = 1 - r i`: if the gaps to 1 sum to less than 1, the product is\nbounded below by a positive constant, so the marginal cannot collapse. This is\nthe sufficient condition the counterexample above shows is missing — and note\nit is checkable from finitely many measured terms *plus* a bound on the tail,\nnever from the measured terms alone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.ratio_rising_to_one_insufficient","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.ratio_rising_to_one_insufficient","kind":"theorem","line":150,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L150-L164","doc":"**The refutation.** There is a ratio sequence that is strictly increasing,\nlies strictly in `(0,1)`, has `1 - r k → 0`, and whose partial products are\n`1/(n+1)` — hence tend to `0`. So \"the ratios rise monotonically toward 1\"\ndoes **not** license \"the marginal converges to a nonzero limit\".\n\nThis is stated as an explicit existential so it can be cited as the reason that\ninference is unavailable, rather than re-derived each time. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.cex_prodUpTo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.cex_prodUpTo","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L138-L148","doc":"The partial products telescope exactly: `∏_{i<n} (i+1)/(i+2) = 1/(n+1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.cex_strictMono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.cex_strictMono","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L124-L136","doc":"Strictly increasing, proved through the `1 - 1/(k+2)` form rather than by a\ncross-multiplication lemma, whose name has moved between Mathlib versions. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.one_sub_cex","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.one_sub_cex","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L116-L122","doc":"`1 - cex k = 1/(k+2)`, so the gaps to 1 are the harmonic tail — **not\nsummable**, which is precisely why the product still dies. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.cex_lt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.cex_lt_one","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L111-L114","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.cex_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.cex_pos","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L105-L109","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.cex","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.cex","kind":"def","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L102-L103","doc":"The counterexample ratio sequence `r k = (k+1)/(k+2) = 1 - 1/(k+2)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.prod_le_pow_of_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.prod_le_pow_of_le","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L85-L95","doc":"If every ratio is nonnegative and at most `c`, the partial products are at\nmost `c ^ n`. With `c < 1` this is geometric decay to zero: a **scope-(b)**\nexclusion whose invariant is the uniform bound `c`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.prodUpTo_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.prodUpTo_succ","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L77-L79","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.prodUpTo_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.prodUpTo_zero","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L74-L75","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.prodUpTo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.MarginalRatio.prodUpTo","kind":"def","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/MarginalRatioExtensivity.lean#L70-L72","doc":"Partial product of a ratio sequence: `∏_{i<n} r i`. Applied to\n`r i = m(i+1)/m(i)` this is `m(n)/m(0)`, so its limit is the marginal's. ","signatureChunk":0}]}